id: k8s.ingress
kind: kubernetes
title: Ingress
aliases: ingress, http routing
tags: networking, http
questions:
  - how do you route HTTP traffic by host and path into a cluster?
invariant: Ingress is L7 routing rules evaluated by an ingress controller that terminates the connection.
answer_30s: Ingress declares host/path routing to Services; an ingress controller (nginx, Traefik, Envoy Gateway) watches those objects and actually routes the traffic.
answer_2m: |
  Two halves, and the interview question is usually about the second: the
  Ingress object is a declaration with no effect on its own. Without a
  controller, nothing routes. The controller is also where TLS termination,
  header rewriting, timeouts and rate limits live. If the requirement goes
  beyond routing (traffic splitting, mTLS, retries per route), the current
  answer is the Gateway API rather than more Ingress annotations.
when_to_use:
  - HTTP(S) entry point, host/path routing, TLS termination
avoid_when:
  - non-HTTP protocols (use Service types) or fine-grained mesh policy (use Gateway API/mesh)
commands:
  - kubectl get ingress -A => which hosts/paths are published
  - kubectl describe ingress api => which backend service and port a rule points at
  - kubectl logs -n ingress-nginx deploy/ingress-nginx-controller => why a request returned 502/404
failure_modes:
  - no ingress controller installed, so the object is inert
  - backend Service or targetPort wrong -> 502/503
  - probe/health path not excluded from auth, so health checks fail
related:
  - k8s.service
  - k8s.service.loadbalancer
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/ingress/
